join detective_thinking right
detective_thinking: I've arrived at the manor of none other than Count Dracula.
detective_thinking: I'm here to investigate his untimely homicide.
detective_thinking: As reported, Dracula was just wrapping up a dinner party when the incident happened.
detective_thinking: There are five suspects in this case, all currently on the scene.
detective_thinking: I don't have to worry about them trying anything mischievous now that I'm here. My incredible monocle is too intimidating. :)
detective_thinking: When I come near a person or a piece of evidence, I can use the [E] key to interact with it.
detective_thinking: I'm going to gather evidence and question the suspects until I figure out exactly what happened here.
detective_thinking: Anything I find will go in my journal, which I can open and close by pressing [TAB].
detective_thinking: My journal will also keep a log of the conversations I've had, so I can reference them later. Some parts might be highlighted for containing important information that I might want to cite later.
detective_thinking: The last tab in my journal also includes some helpful notes about vampires, at least some of which will no doubt come in handy for this investigation.
detective_thinking: When I question someone, I will do so by selecting one or more records from my journal to ask about.
detective_thinking: If I want to know someone's thoughts about something, I can select that one item when questioning them.
detective_thinking: If I want to use multiple pieces of evidence (including physical evidence, conversation transcripts, or reference book entries) to draw a conclusion, then I can select those multiple items when questioning someone.
detective_thinking: I usually won't need to combine more than two pieces of evidence at a time, though it may be necessary at some point. But any more than three probably won't get me anywhere.
detective_thinking: Alright, I'm ready. Let's investigate.
